    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `VersionControl.get_base_rev_args`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `get_base_rev_args` function is a static method defined within the `VersionControl` class in the `pip._internal.vcs.versioncontrol` module. This function is intended to return a list of command-line arguments that specify a base revision for version control systems (VCS) commands. The purpose of this function is to abstract the details of how different VCS systems handle revision arguments, allowing for a unified interface for specifying revisions across various VCS backends.\n\nThe function takes a single parameter, `rev`, which is a string representing the name of a revision to install. This parameter cannot be `None`, as the function expects a valid revision identifier. The function is designed to be overridden by subclasses of `VersionControl` that are specific to different VCS systems, such as Git, Subversion, or Mercurial. Each subclass would implement its own version of `get_base_rev_args` to return the appropriate command-line arguments for that VCS.\n\nSince this is a static method, it does not rely on any instance-specific data or methods. Instead, it is meant to be called on the class itself, not on an instance of the class. The function does not have any side effects; it simply returns a list of strings without modifying any external state or performing any I/O operations.\n\nThe function raises a `NotImplementedError` if it is called directly on the `VersionControl` class without being overridden by a subclass. This indicates that the method is intended to be abstract and must be implemented by any concrete subclass that represents a specific VCS system. There are no other error handling mechanisms or early returns in the function itself, as its primary purpose is to be overridden and implemented by subclasses.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
